<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Munqidhite Emirate of Shaizar</h1><p class="st-meta">1081 CE – 1157 CE · capital Shaizar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Arab emirate on the Orontes that survived between Crusaders and Muslim powers and produced the memoirist Usama ibn Munqidh.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Munqidh, an Arab family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kinana">Kinana</a>, held lands near Shaizar on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes</a>. In 1081 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-munqidh">Ali ibn Munqidh</a> acquired the fortress of Shaizar from its Byzantine-appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and made it the seat of a family <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Shaizar's castle on a ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes</a> was its strength. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> paid tribute or fought as needed against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari Ismailis</a> and Turkish rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and withstood a siege by the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> and Frankish allies in 1138. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usama-ibn-munqidh">Usama ibn Munqidh</a>, born there in 1095, wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitab-al-itibar">Kitab al-I'tibar</a>, vivid memoirs of hunting, war and Frankish customs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1157 a great earthquake destroyed the citadel during a family celebration and killed nearly all the Munqidhites. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a> took the ruins and rebuilt the fortress.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1081 CE – 1157 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.21%;width:1.51%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1081 CE – 1157 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shaizar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Munqidh, Sultan ibn Munqidh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Munqidh, Shaizar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1119&amp;focus=munqidhite-emirate-of-shaizar" aria-label="Munqidhite Emirate of Shaizar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5605 903 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Munqidhite Emirate of Shaizar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5925 1159L6481 1127M5925 1159L5936 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5925 1159h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5925 1159h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shaizar. <a href="/atlas?year=1119&amp;focus=munqidhite-emirate-of-shaizar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

